# TablePH VIP Console - Environment Configuration Template
#
# This is a project-level template. Actual environment variables should be set
# in server/.env (backend) and frontend/.env (frontend).
#
# NEVER commit real .env files to version control.

# Backend Environment Variables (server/.env)
# ===========================================
# MONGODB_URI - MongoDB connection string (required)
# PORT - Server port (default: 8787, Render sets this automatically)
# NODE_ENV - Environment: development or production
# SESSION_SECRET - Session secret (required in production, use long random string)
# SESSION_MAX_AGE_MS - Session duration in milliseconds (default: 12 hours)
# COOKIE_SECURE - Set to 'true' in production with HTTPS
# COOKIE_SAMESITE - Cookie same-site policy: 'lax' (single-origin) or 'none' (cross-origin)
# CLIENT_ORIGIN - Frontend URL for CORS (leave empty for single-origin deployment)
# LOGIN_WINDOW_MS - Login rate limit window in milliseconds
# LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS - Max login attempts before rate limit
# OPERATION_CONCURRENCY - Max parallel operations across all users (default: 3)
# ADMIN_USERNAME - Initial admin username
# ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD - Initial admin password (change on first login)
# MEMBER_USERNAME - Initial regular user username
# MEMBER_INITIAL_PASSWORD - Initial regular user password (change on first login)
# CREDENTIAL_TTL_MS - In-memory credential store TTL (default: 7 days)

# Frontend Environment Variables (frontend/.env)
# ==============================================
# VITE_API_BASE_URL - Backend API URL
#   Development: /api (uses Vite proxy)
#   Production: https://radeon-table.onrender.com/api (or your deployed backend URL)

# Deployment Instructions
# ======================
#
# PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT (Cross-Origin: Netlify Frontend + Render Backend)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. Backend (Render):
#    - Create a new Web Service on Render
#    - Connect your GitHub repository
#    - Build Command: npm install
#    - Start Command: node server/index.js
#    - Environment Variables:
#      * MONGODB_URI: Your MongoDB Atlas connection string
#      * SESSION_SECRET: Generate a long random string (e.g., openssl rand -base64 32)
#      * NODE_ENV: production
#      * COOKIE_SECURE: true
#      * COOKIE_SAMESITE: none
#      * CLIENT_ORIGIN: Your Netlify frontend URL (e.g., https://your-app.netlify.app)
#      * ADMIN_USERNAME: Your desired admin username
#      * ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD: Strong initial password (change on first login)
#      * MEMBER_USERNAME: Your desired regular user username
#      * MEMBER_INITIAL_PASSWORD: Strong initial password (change on first login)
#
# 2. Frontend (Netlify):
#    - Create a new site from Git on Netlify
#    - Build settings:
#      * Build command: npm run build
#      * Publish directory: frontend/dist
#      * Base directory: (leave blank)
#    - Environment Variables:
#      * VITE_API_BASE_URL: https://your-backend.onrender.com/api
#    - The netlify.toml file in the root handles SPA routing automatically
#
# 3. Post-Deployment:
#    - Log in as the admin user and change the initial password
#    - Set up regular users with appropriate trial quotas and access durations
#    - Configure MongoDB Atlas IP whitelist if needed (allow Render's IP range)
#
# DEVELOPMENT SETUP
# ================
#
# 1. Install dependencies:
#    npm run install:backend
#    npm run install:frontend
#
# 2. Configure backend (server/.env):
#    Copy server/.env.example to server/.env and fill in values
#    - Set MONGODB_URI to your local MongoDB or Atlas connection
#    - Set SESSION_SECRET to any string for development
#    - Leave CLIENT_ORIGIN empty for local development
#
# 3. Configure frontend (frontend/.env):
#    Copy frontend/.env.example to frontend/.env
#    - Set VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api (uses Vite proxy)
#
# 4. Run development servers:
#    npm run dev:api  (starts backend on port 8787)
#    npm run dev:web  (starts frontend on port 5173)
#    Or use: npm run dev (starts both concurrently)
#
# SINGLE-ORIGIN DEPLOYMENT (Alternative)
# ======================================
#
# If you prefer to serve both frontend and backend from the same origin:
# - Build the frontend: npm run build
# - Express will automatically serve frontend/dist at the root
# - Set COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax and leave CLIENT_ORIGIN empty
# - No CORS configuration needed
# - This is simpler but requires the backend to serve static files
